    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d448">

                <pb facs="0669" n="653" sameAs="frus:frus1947v04/pg653"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24/1–247</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Chief of the Division of Lend-Lease and
                        Surplus War-Property Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Matlock</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to Mr. Henry Waegelein, Deputy Assistant to the United
                        States Maritime Commission</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-01-02">January 2,
                        1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Waegelein</hi>: This is in response to
                    your telephone request of Mr. Truesdell of this office for advice regarding
                    action taken by the Department of State to effect the return of merchant
                    vessels, use and custody of which were transferred to the Government of the
                    Union of Soviet Socialist Republics under the Lend-Lease Act.</p>
                <p>On October 31, 1946 the Under Secretary for Economic Affairs<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d448-f1"/> called in the Soviet Chargé d’Affaires<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d448-f2"/> and in the course of conversation <pb facs="0670" n="654"
                        xml:id="pg654"/> handed him an <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi><anchor n="4" xml:id="d448-f3"/> which reviewed the note
                    of the United States Government of September 14, 1946<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d448-f4"/> and asked when a reply might be expected. In reviewing
                    the note of September 14 reference was made to merchant vessels as follows:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“This note reaffirmed the position of the Government of the United States, as
                        set forth in its note of March 18, 1946<anchor n="6" xml:id="d448-f5"/> that
                        the purchase of dry-cargo vessels, tankers, and other merchant vessels, use
                        and custody of which were transferred to the Government of the Union of
                        Soviet Socialist Republics under the Lend-Lease Act, is governed by existing
                        statutes of the United States and that those not purchased must be returned
                        to the Government of the United States in conformity with such statutes. The
                        Government of the United States expressed its desire that discussion of the
                        disposition of these vessels also commence on or before October 15,
                        1946.”</p>
                </quote>
                <p>We shall be pleased to inform you from time to time of further developments in
                    this regard.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Clifford</hi> C. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Matlock</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
